scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#desert-psych","chapters":["Sección 5.14"],"pages":[[1,171,null]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":171,"text":"…Green On Red became at best worthy heirs to the Band, at worst faceless dispensers of \"blue-collar rock\". In Arizona, Howe Gelb, the brain behind Giant Sand (3), came up with an original and quirky fusion of rock, country and psychedelia. Not so much prolific as unfocused, Gelb too wasted his talent over…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":171,"text":"…section of bassist Joey Burns and drummer John Convertino (the future Calexico line-up), suddenly opted for Neil Young's abrasive country-rock. Giant Sand's masterpiece was probably the 25-minute jam BBQ Suite, off the largely improvised Backyard Barbecue Broadcast (1995), which stood as a summary of…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":250,"text":"…country with a voice reminiscent of Townes Van Zandt on Bloomed (1995) and particularly on the concept album Devotion And Doubt (1997), backed by Giant Sand and Marc Ribot. Protagonists of the country-rock renaissance included: in Seattle Gerald Collier (1), with the agonizing I Had To Laugh Like Hell…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":254,"text":"…(1) emerged out of the alt-country legion with her third album, Blacklisted (2002), featuring Shadowy Men On A Shadowy Planet's Brian Connelly and Giant Sand's Howe Gelb, Joey Burns and John Convertino. British soundscapes, 1993-99 However, the most influential female singer-songwriter of the 1990s was…"}],"podcast":[],"awards":[],"concerts":[],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":200933321810446,"type":"album","name":"Valley of Rain","list_id":225077317735581,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Cowpunk","src":"musthear","rank":19,"year":1985,"note":null,"yt":"rfvnb6Iw4bs","scaruffi":null},{"id":2759432